/// =====================================================================================
/// sm_payments — model-level settings
///
/// Three settings here carry actual design weight. The rest is boilerplate.
///
/// 1. **directLakeBehavior: DirectLakeOnly**
///    The default (`Automatic`) silently falls back to DirectQuery when a Direct Lake
///    guardrail is breached — too many rows in a table, an unsupported data type, a view
///    behind the table, a column the engine cannot page in. The query still returns, just
///    slower, and nobody finds out until someone complains about a report. `DirectLakeOnly`
///    makes that breach an error instead.
///
///    That is the same choice this repo makes everywhere else: a DQ rule at `error` severity
///    fails the run rather than logging and continuing; the T-SQL linter fails the build
///    rather than warning. A performance cliff that announces itself is worth more than one
///    that hides, because the hidden one gets discovered by a user and the loud one gets
///    discovered by CI.
///
///    The cost is real and worth stating: on a capacity under memory pressure, a report that
///    would have degraded now breaks. That is the right trade for a model this size and the
///    wrong one for a 10-billion-row fact, where `Automatic` plus monitoring is the honest
///    answer. Naming the threshold matters more than picking a side.
///
/// 2. **No calculated columns, and no calculated tables, anywhere in this model.**
///    Not a stylistic preference — Direct Lake tables do not support them. This is why
///    `dim_date` is generated by `src/warehouse/procs/01_sp_load_dim_date.sql` into a real
///    warehouse table rather than by `CALENDAR()` in DAX, and why every additive flag
///    (`is_approved`, `is_declined`, ...) is materialised as a `smallint` on the fact by
///    `procs/07_sp_load_fact_transaction.sql` rather than computed here. The modelling constraint
///    reached back and changed the warehouse DDL, which is the sort of thing that only shows
///    up if you design both layers together.
///
/// 3. **defaultPowerBIDataSourceVersion: powerBI_V3** and `discourageImplicitMeasures`.
///    The second one is the load-bearing half: it stops a report author dragging
///    `distinct_account_count` onto a card and getting an implicit SUM. Every number in the
///    report then comes from a measure in `measures.dax` that someone reviewed.
///
/// UNVALIDATED — see semantic-model/README.md.
/// =====================================================================================
model Model
	culture: en-GB
	defaultPowerBIDataSourceVersion: powerBI_V3
	discourageImplicitMeasures
	directLakeBehavior: DirectLakeOnly
	sourceQueryCulture: en-GB
	dataAccessOptions
		legacyRedirects
		returnErrorValuesAsNull

	annotation __PBI_TimeIntelligenceEnabled = 0

	annotation PBI_QueryOrder = ["DatabaseQuery"]

	annotation PBI_ProTooling = ["TMDL"]

	ref table dim_date
	ref table dim_account
	ref table dim_customer
	ref table dim_merchant
	ref table dim_card_product
	ref table dim_currency
	ref table dim_decline_reason
	ref table fact_transaction
	ref table fact_dispute
	ref table agg_merchant_daily

	ref expression DatabaseQuery
